      "description": "At least one of PAD, diabetes mellitus, or lower-extremity wound, on condition_occurrence. WINDOWS ARE PER-INDICATION and were tightened 2026-08-12 from the unbounded window inherited from 1797941: PAD and DM are chronic, so any prior documentation counts and the window runs to +1 year (the amputation admission is frequently where PAD is first formally coded); a wound is an acute episode, so it is bracketed to +/-1 year -- a wound five years earlier is not this amputation's indication."
    },
    {
      "name": "No limb-trauma mechanism during the index admission",
      "description": "Zero crush / burn / degloving / fracture records ATTACHED TO THE INDEX INPATIENT VISIT (RestrictVisit). Concepts: 194229 crushing injury of lower limb, 72487 crushing injury of foot, 197751 burn of lower limb, 4095264 degloving injury, 4187096 fracture of lower limb -- 1,341 standard concepts expanded, all [vocab query] verified 2026-08-12. REPLACED 4096479 'Traumatic amputation' on 2026-08-12: that concept's descendants are 'Traumatic amputation above knee / below knee / of foot', the same words a coder may use for the SURGICAL amputation just performed, so a window spanning the admission excluded 220 of 935 real Duke patients (24%) who had all already passed the PAD/DM/wound rule. Mechanism concepts cannot be confused with a surgical amputation, which is why this criterion can sit ON the admission (strongest signal) rather than being pushed strictly-before to dodge the ambiguity. GENERAL injury ancestors were REJECTED after measuring the damage: SNOMED files open wounds under injuries, so 440921 'Traumatic injury' shares 888 of this study's 1,372 dysvascular wound concepts and 4130852 'Injury of lower limb' shares 982. Either would have excluded the dysvascular patients this study is about. FRACTURE IS INCLUDED (4187096, added at the study team's direction and dominating the set at 1,088 of the 1,341 concepts). Its 225-concept overlap with the wound indication is entirely via OPEN fractures and is INTENDED: an open fracture is a traumatic wound, and such a patient belongs outside a dysvascular cohort rather than being retained by the wound rule. ACCEPTED COST: 4187096 also spans CLOSED fractures including hip, so an elderly patient admitted after a fall who also undergoes a dysvascular amputation on that same admission is now excluded; visit-scoping limits but does not remove that. If the next run's attrition looks too blunt, 4177494 'Open fracture of lower limb' (225 descendants) is the narrower substitute. An earlier draft of this description said fracture had been rejected -- that predated the decision to add it and was self-contradictory; corrected here.",
